# {{project_name}} — Detailed plan: {{initiative_title}}

> Executable backlog of {{initiative_short}}. Strategy and rationale live in
> `master-plan.md`; the calendar schedule in `timeframe-plan.md`; wave state in
> `task.md`; the per-wave prompts in `execute.md` §7.

## 0. Conventions

- **Task ID**: `T-<WAVE>-<n>`. **Status**: ☐ pending · 🔄 in progress · ⛔ blocked · ✅ done.
- **Repo aliases**: {{alias}} = {{full_repo_path_or_url}}
- **Cross-repo ownership — by COUPLING, never by consumer**: every artifact a task
  creates lives in the repo whose surface it is coupled to, not in the repo that
  consumes it. Schema-coupled SQL (fixtures, demo seeds, psql scripts) lives with the
  schema/migrations repo even when its only consumer is the UI — whoever migrates the
  schema must see it, or it rots silently in another repo. A script that imports code
  from an app (e.g. a parity check importing the app's own lib) lives in that app's
  repo, pointing at the other repo's fixture in its header. (Lesson learned: a
  schema fixture born in the frontend repo had to be moved post-review.)
- **Code legibility (no indentation hell)**: keep code flat and readable, not just
  correct — the diff of every task must read top-to-bottom. Max ~3-4 nesting levels;
  use early returns / guard clauses instead of nested `if/else`; extract named helpers
  (e.g. `ensureUserId()`, `insertMenu(parentId, items)`) instead of 4-5-level loops in
  scripts/seeders, and split deep JSX into subcomponents instead of `<div>` + nested
  className ternaries; flatten nested ternaries. Exclude deeply-nested HTML (emails with
  table + MSO conditionals) from the auto-formatter if it worsens them (`.prettierignore`).
- **Reuse before writing**: logic that already exists is consumed, not rewritten;
  logic two or more components use or need is extracted ONCE into a shared helper both
  consume (rule of TWO). Guard: extract only when both consumers would change together
  (same-behavior test) — when they are expected to diverge, duplicate deliberately and
  record the why. The helper lives in the project's shared root, never inside one
  consumer; across repos, in a shared package when one exists, else in the repo owning
  the surface it is coupled to (see the ownership convention above), consumed through
  the existing dependency direction; when no dependency direction exists between the
  two repos, duplicate deliberately and record the why in the `task.md` logbook. The decision is made in PLANNING and recorded in
  the task's Scope manifest (`Reuse:`), never improvised at execution time — and the
  helper plus the rewiring of ALL its current consumers is ONE task, which every
  consuming task lists under `Depends on`.
- **Comments explain the WHY, never the plan**: a comment earns its place when it
  explains non-obvious logic (an invariant, a workaround for a named external bug, a
  gotcha, a non-obvious algorithm), documents a public API in this repo's existing
  convention, or is a TODO pointing at the issue tracker. Never restate what the code
  does, narrate decisions or rationale ("we chose X because"), narrate changes ("added
  in wave 2", "moved from"), reference the plan (task IDs, wave numbers, execution-doc
  names) or leave commented-out code. Decisions already live in `master-plan.md`, in
  these task cards and in the `task.md` logbook; a comment repeating them is a second
  record that rots.
- **Scope manifests**: every task card carries a `Scope manifest` — the structured
  output of the analysis (files + why + impact + reuse + census). It DEFINES the task's
  scope — the shared helper and the consumers it names are inside it — and it is the
  POSITIVE scope:
  wave prompts inline it as `# SCOPE` so executing sessions never re-derive it. It is
  refreshed at every wave close (execute.md §2b) with what was ACTUALLY touched, so
  later waves inherit fresh state.
- **Executor per task**: `sonnet` by default (from an executor brief, execute.md
  §1.8); `opus` on ⚠gate waves. Recorded in every card as `Executor:`; the
  orchestrator never implements first (escalation ladder, execute.md §1.7). The
  executor is not the last hand on the code: every wave ends with one code-simplifier
  pass over its integrated diff, run by the orchestrator (execute.md §1.11).
- **Base DoD** (applies to EVERY task, on top of its own DoD):
  - [ ] {{repo_quality_command — e.g. lint/typecheck/tests of that repo}} → exit 0
  - [ ] No files touched outside the task's scope (the scope IS this card's Scope
    manifest: the shared helper and its named consumers are inside it)
  - [ ] No added comment narrates the plan: `git diff {{base_branch}}...HEAD -- ':!*.md'
    ':!{{docs_dir}}' | grep -E '^[+][^+]' | grep -E '(//|#|/[*]|^[+][[:space:]]*[*]|--|<!--)'
    | grep -E '(T-W[0-9]+-[0-9]+|(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])W[0-9]+([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)|master-plan|detailed-plan|timeframe-plan|executive-proposal|execute\.md|task\.md)'
    | wc -l | tr -d ' '` → `0` (BSD and GNU grep; `wc -l` because `grep -c` exits 1 on a
    clean diff; narration vs why stays a judgement call for the regression review and
    the simplifier, never for grep)
  - [ ] No secrets in code, commits or docs
  - [ ] Conventional commit(s), atomic, referencing the task ID

